  "query_preview": "..\nAs of 305 AM EDT Sunday...\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Lingering light rain is possible on the Eastern Shore, dry \nweather elsewhere today. A warming trend will bring above average \nhighs in the mid to upper 80s by late week.\n\nThe impressive Nor'easter will weaken around the NJ coast today, \ncontinuing to bring impacts, albeit fewer impacts than the last few \ndays. Coastal flooding for coastal areas (see key message 2), light \nrain chances for the Eastern Shore, and breezy winds from a well \nmixed atmosphere will continue today. Early this morning, light rain \nwas noted on the radar across coastal portions of the area. High-res \nmodels show the light rain dissipating over most of the area except \nfor the Eastern Shore. Chc PoPs over the MD Eastern Shore and slight \nchc PoPs over the VA Eastern Shore continue into late afternoon, \ndecreasing to slight chc PoPs across only the MD Eastern Shore \novernight. With the lingering precip, highs will likely not make it \nout of the 60s on the Eastern Shore. For the rest of the area, dry \nweather is expected with temperatures ranging from the upper 60s in \nthe Northern Neck and Middle Peninsula to the upper 70s in south \ncentral VA and NE NC. Low level flow will shift out of the WNW with \ngusts to 30 mph across the area, as the atmosphere becomes well \nmixed. The highest gusts will be observed in the late afternoon.\n\nThe low will continue to weaken and track further NE along the New \nEngland coast this week. Dry weather will return for the entire area \nMonday, lasting through much of the week. The placement of the low \non Monday will allow temperatures to remain in the 70s across the \narea, with lower 70s on the Eastern Shore and Northern Neck to upper \n70s in NE NC. High pressure moves over the area on Tuesday before \npushing offshore Wednesday, as upper ridging builds over the E/SE \nCONUS. This will lead to a warming trend with mainly dry wx over for \nthe rest of the week. Above average temperatures in the mid to upper \n80s are possible by Thu/Fri.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Tides begin to subside today, though minor to \nlocally moderate to major flooding across the middle Chesapeake Bay \nwill linger through this morning's high tide cycle. Flooding remains \nelevated over the Bay side of the MD Eastern Shore, with minor \ncoastal to locally moderate flooding persisting into Monday.\n\nThe nor'easter continues to lift farther north, lingering off the \nNJ/Long Island coast early this morning. Tidal anomalies have begun \nto ease along the western shore as winds shift offshore, but remain \n1-2 ft above normal astronomical tide levels. This will produce \nanother round of widespread minor to moderate coastal flooding this \nmorning. Coastal Flood Warnings remain in effect for the tidal \nPotomac, Rappahannock, and upper James rivers, as well as Virginia \nBeach and the Bay side of the MD/VA Eastern Shore. Moderate to \nlocally major coastal flooding is forecast with this morning's high \ntide cycle, concentrated along the middle Chesapeake Bay Eastern \nShore as gusty NW winds pile water along the eastern side of the \nbay. Conditions notably improve Sun evening into Mon as winds \ndiminish and offshore flow enhances tidal drainage during ebb \ncycles. Nevertheless, additional rounds of nuisance to minor coastal \nflooding (and Coastal Flood Advisories) are expected into Mon \nevening for the MD zones.",
